<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syrian civil war</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2011">2011</a>–<a href="/years/2024">2024</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Syrian civil war was an armed conflict that grew out of the Syrian revolution of March 2011, when the Ba'athist regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashar-al-assad">Bashar al-Assad</a> met pro-democracy protests with lethal force and the uprising turned into civil war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezbollah">Hezbollah</a> backed Assad, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and a United States-led program armed rebel groups; in 2014 the Islamic State seized eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">Chemical weapons</a> were used predominantly by government forces. By the end of the decade the war had caused an estimated 470,000–610,000 violent deaths. It culminated in December 2024, when Hay'at Tahrir al-Sham took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> and Assad fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Work at the site was halted by the <b>Syrian civil war</b> in 2011.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Since 2011 the site has suffered heavy looting during the <b>Syrian civil war</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Syria the arrest and abuse of children in Daraa for writing anti-government slogans set off protests that met harsh clampdowns and grew into the <b>Syrian civil war</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/arab-spring">Arab Spring · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Arab Spring and the <b>Syrian civil war</b> its Iraqi remnants grew into the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant, which most Islamists reject as blasphemous.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/islamism">Islamism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Many Arab artists have recorded it anew; Elissa, Christina Sawaya and Faia Younan sang it in a video clip depicting the <b>Syrian civil war</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/mawtini">My Homeland</a></li></ul></section>
